  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n    For this minimization problem, we should consider sequences that are \"pulsed\" or \"block-like\" rather than smoothly varying. These sequences create clear discontinuities that are effective for energy concentration. We will optimize a sequence consisting of periodic square wave segments, explicitly targeting non-zero convolution values while avoiding the introduction of overly complex features like multiple frequencies until we have achieved a good baseline.\n\n    Mathematical specification:\n    We represent the sequence as a piecewise constant function \\(a(t)\\) defined over \\(t \\in [0,1]\\). \n\n    Let \\(a = \\{a_0, a_1, \\dots, a_{n-1}\\}\\) where \\(a_i = 0\\) if \\(i\\) is not near a specified peak index \\(p\\), and \\(a_i = v\\) otherwise. Here, \\(p\\) and \\(v\\) are parameters we will optimize with tighter focusing than the previous strategy.\n\n    This creates a sequence that is:\n        - Valued \\( p \\) at its peak positions\n        - Otherwise zero (or minimal values for testing)\n\n    Constraints:\n        - Each \\(a_i \\geq 0\\)\n        - Sum \\( \\geq 0.01 \\) (we'll enforce non-zero sum)\n        - All values \\( \\leq 1000 \\)\n\n    We use an algebraic transformation:\n        Let \\( p \\in \\{0,1,2\\ldots,511\\} \\) (index) \n        let \\( \\sigma \\in [0.01,1000] \\) (scaling factor)\n        let \\( k = 0.7 \\times \\text{length} \\) (fraction of sequence where we set high values)\n\n    But note: we cannot assume any predefined frequency structure.\n\n    Implementation:\n    We'll implement a sequence generator that creates \\(N\\) spikes (program-controlled) at random positions, then normalizes the entire sequence by its sum.\n\n    Steps:\n\n    1. Initialize parameters: (p_opt: index, \u03c3_opt: scale, write_col: enables periodic spikes)\n\n    We'll set the sequence generation phases explicitly:\n\n        Phase 1: Single peak (focus on one clear region)\n        Phase 2: Two peaks (with minimum separation)\n        Phase 3: Three peaks (with multiple positions)\n\n    2. Generate candidates for each phase:\n\n        For single peak (Phase1):\n            - Place a single peak starting at a probable target frequency\n            - Let tile_length = probability floor. For example, 10 iterations per initial candidate\n\n        For two peaks (Phase2):\n            - Place two peaks separated by at least 1/10 of the sequence length\n            - The separation must ensure distinct discontinuities\n\n        For three peaks (Phase3):\n            - Place three peaks with roughly equally spaced positions\n            - Use greater length-to-width matching for the peaks\n\n    3. Optimization:\n\n        For each candidate configuration (defined by its peak positions and value), we compute:\n\n            seq = array of length n=512\n            sum_a = seq.sum()\n            If sum_a < 0.01, we increase the values to make sure valid.\n\n            convolve the sequence via FFT to compute M = max convolution value\n\n            J = (2*n*M) / (sum_a)^2\n\n        Then we track the best J value across all configurations.\n\n    4. Iterations:\n\n        - For each phase, generate ridges via minimal separation and varying phases\n        - Update through gradient-free optimization or direct replacement of the sequence\n        - Use a descent instead of random walks to move between configurations\n\n    Search choices and variations:\n\n        We'll use:\n\n        - Two new optimization methods: activeset expansion and blocking\n        - Bayesian-like inference (using past scores to predict improvements)\n        - Random initialization with fallback to unimodal optimization\n\n        Steps for initialization:\n\n            Initialize a=\"multi_peak\"\n\n            We'll try with 100 initial peaks.\n\n            Phase configuration:\n\n                single_peak_step = 10-50 (depends on number of peaks)\n\n            We'll use:\n\n                Generate random peaks: spread uniformly within [0, n) for n=100 peaks initially.\n\n                Allowed peak positions: as length indices\n\n                Start with one peak (n=1) and n_max (say 10) and normalize.\n\n            Optimization iteration parameters:\n\n                max_single_peak_repetitions = 5 (each single peak can be generated 5 times)\n\n                max_double_repetitions = 5\n\n                etc.\n\n    Compute budget and robustness:\n\n        We'll calculate:\n\n            time_per_evaluation = ceil(500 * n / 1000) for FFT and other operations\n\n        We can reduce by sub-sampling or using cheaper convolution - but we are bounded to use IEEE floating point and standard libraries.\n\n        We should ensure:\n\n            Series of ones for minimal sequence: time of evaluation will be only O(n)\n\n            Use FFT for convolution computations to keep operations O(n log n)\n\n        Time management:\n\n            Deadline = 1500 seconds\n\n            We'll plan:\n\n                For each peak configuration type (single, double, triple), we allocate:\n\n                    time_budget_phase = min(35%, 10%) of total time? More realistically, we'll allocate based on phase number.\n\n            For each candidate sequence:\n\n                We'll compute J once (via the evaluate_sequence function) and track by updated J values.\n\n            The fallback sequence is the one with best J from the previous parent.\n\n    Validation and return contract:\n\n        - Only compare sequences using the official evaluate_sequence function.\n        - Ensure every update sequence is non-negative.\n        - The original objective function is used directly.\n\n        sequence = [float(x) for x in sequence] from the generator.\n\n        Output formats: any.\n\n        Return the best sequence found with length n=512 (using periodic boundary).",
